### Using the information to solve

In order to find $b'(t_0)$ we need to find $a(t_0)$. Using the Pythagorean theorem and the fact that $b(t_0)=6$ and $c=10$, we can find that $a(t_0)=8$.


Let's plug $\blueD{a(t_0)}=\blueD{8}$, $\maroonD{a'(t_0)}=\maroonD{-3}$, and $\goldD{b(t_0)}=\goldD{6}$ into the expression for $b'(t_0)$:

$\begin{align}
b'(t_0)&=-\dfrac{\blueD{a(t_0)}\maroonD{a'(t_0)}}{\goldD{b(t_0)}}
\\\\
&=-\dfrac{(\blueD{8})(\maroonD{-3})}{(\goldD{6})}
\\\\
&=4
\end{align}$

Now let's plug $\blueD{a(t_0)}=\blueD{8}$, $\maroonD{a'(t_0)}=\maroonD{-3}$, $\goldD{b(t_0)}=\goldD{6}$, and $\purpleC{b'(t_0)}=\purpleC{4}$ into the expression for $A'(t_0)$:

$\begin{align}
A'(t_0)&=\dfrac{\maroonD{a'(t_0)}\goldD{b(t_0)}+\blueD{a(t_0)}\purpleC{b'(t_0)}}{2}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{(\maroonD{-3})(\goldD{6})+(\blueD{8})\left(\purpleC{4}\right)}{2}
\\\\
&=7
\end{align}$
